WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.660
I would consider myself 
a paleoanthropologist,

00:00:03.743 --> 00:00:06.540
so I study humans, but in the past.

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:10.470
We know the fire has been around for a long time, 
at least a million years.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.119
The question is, what's been the evolving relationship that humans and 
human ancestors have had with fire?

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.250
I spent a lot of time surveying 
and leading excavations in Malawi,

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:22.160
which is in 
Southern Central Africa.

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:25.230
We thought it was going 
to tell us something about this

00:00:25.313 --> 00:00:28.469
particular time period where we 
start to see a really, pretty

00:00:28.553 --> 00:00:31.980
substantial increase in the 
complexity with which humans,

00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:36.689
early humans are making stone 
tools and just kind of making different kinds of artifacts.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.960
We knew based on the research 
that had been done by our

00:00:53.043 --> 00:00:55.450
colleagues in sort of 
the geosciences, that there had

00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:58.149
been periods of time in the 
past when that lake was rising

00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:01.810
and falling and at some point 
in time, it was basically just gone.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.219
We were thinking, how would that have affected 
people on the kind of the broad scale.

00:01:11.609 --> 00:01:13.920
Our colleagues, they drilled this massive core down

00:01:14.003 --> 00:01:15.150
into the sediments of the lake.

00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:16.799
They pulled up all of this mud,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.969
and in the mud is this record 
of past vegetation and past fire history,

00:01:21.053 --> 00:01:22.859
because there's little bits of charcoal

00:01:22.943 --> 00:01:25.799
that end up in the system when there's more burning on the landscape.

00:01:26.129 --> 00:01:29.340
They were still busy working 
through the majority of their results,

00:01:29.423 --> 00:01:31.739
at the same time that we started our archaeological project,

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.370
we were working in parallel and sort of overlapping a bit.

00:01:35.453 --> 00:01:37.620
And it wasn't really until we had this, kind of one

00:01:37.703 --> 00:01:42.870
conversation where we realized 
that we were both coming up against the same problem.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.629
We were finding that all of the 
archaeological sites that we

00:01:45.713 --> 00:01:48.329
were discovering dated to a 
very specific time period.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.390
That was all within the last eighty-five thousand years.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.859
They were discovering that they 
were finding a lot of evidence

00:01:55.943 --> 00:01:59.069
for major ecological turnover 
and environmental change that

00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:02.489
they couldn't readily explain 
using climate variables alone.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.459
And that was also 
within the last eighty-five thousand years.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:10.400
So, it kind of wasn't until 
we had a conversation with each other that we knew

00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:11.900
that we had complementary data sets that

00:02:11.983 --> 00:02:13.349
were really going to tell that story together.

00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:29.449
We think a fire often as this
destructive tool.

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:34.159
That doesn't have to be the case, because if 
you have the right kind of ecological knowledge

00:02:34.243 --> 00:02:36.669
and you know exactly where and when to set the fires,

00:02:36.753 --> 00:02:37.610
and how to control them,

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:43.310
what you can actually do is you can shape 
the landscape to do your bidding, in a sense.

00:02:43.393 --> 00:02:44.990
You can clear out undergrowth strategically

00:02:45.073 --> 00:02:46.539
and then you can 
make new grass come up

00:02:46.623 --> 00:02:50.469
and that draws in all of the animals 
that like to hang out in those kinds of habitats,

00:02:50.553 --> 00:02:53.379
so you can sort of engineer the world around you.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.210
What we came out with was, I think,

00:02:56.293 --> 00:02:57.939
a pretty interesting result.

00:02:58.023 --> 00:02:59.960
We were expecting the environment

00:03:00.043 --> 00:03:01.610
and climate to have influenced the people,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.740
but really what we found is that it was

00:03:03.823 --> 00:03:05.840
people who are influencing their environment.

